# LaTeX build artefacts.
#
# Everything here is regenerable from the .tex sources by pdfLaTeX/BibTeX/latexmk.
# The generated PDFs are ignored too: a committed PDF goes stale silently, and a
# stale manuscript PDF is worse than no PDF. Rebuild rather than commit.
#
# NOT ignored, and must stay tracked: *.tex, references.bib, figures/*.pdf,
# make_figures.py, README.md. The figures are inputs to the build, not outputs of it.
#
# ...with two named exceptions, below. Two figures embed MRI slices and are therefore
# fastMRI-DERIVED IMAGE DATA. The NYU fastMRI data sharing agreement, paragraph 5(c),
# forbids distributing "any portion or all of the fastMRI Dataset or any subsequent
# variables or data files derived from" it, and carves out only "uses in academic
# publications and presentations with citations as provided in paragraph 6". A figure
# inside a submitted manuscript is inside that carve-out. A file in a public GitHub
# repository is not: it is unbounded redistribution to any person or entity.
#
# The same rule already governs pipeline_out/ (ignored whole, for the same reason).
# Derived STATISTICS may be committed -- that is what fig1-fig5, figS1 and figS2 are.
# Derived IMAGES may not. Ship these two to the journal out of band; make_figures.py
# rebuilds them from pipeline_out/cache/*.h5 in one command.
figures/fig6_qualitative_phase.pdf
figures/figS3_qualitative_cohorts.pdf

